                    ], style="spark-research-page", attributes=[attr("href", "assets/history/spark-paper.pdf"), attr("target", "_blank"), attr("aria-label", "Open the original Spark research paper")]),
                    group([
                        span("HOTCLOUD ’10", style="archive-tag"),
                        rich_heading([
                            text("Cluster Computing"),
                            line_break(),
                            text("with Working Sets"),
                        ], level=2),
                        paragraph([
                            text("The ideas are published."),
                            line_break(),
                            text("The code is open."),
                            line_break(),
                            text("Others can build on it."),
                        ]),
                        image("assets/history/spark.png", "Apache Spark", style="open-source-spark"),
                    ]),
                ], style="open-source-story"),
            ], style="history-visual"),
            prose("Spark combines distributed execution with data reuse, including keeping working data in memory.", style="history-backstory"),
        ], style="history-scene"),
            prose("Chronological milestones · spacing not to scale", style="history-scale-note"),
            node(NodeKind.Footer, children=[
            span("Danny’s Code Corner"),
            span("15"),
        ]),
        ],
        notes="""Visual assets: see assets/history/CREDITS.md. Official logos identify the projects, not sponsorship. The Berkeley photo was taken in 2013 and illustrates the location, not the founding event. 2010 — Spark goes open source.

The 2010 paper explains the working-set motivation. Memory reuse is a capability, not a requirement that every dataset fits in RAM; Spark still reads, writes, shuffles and spills. This connects to the cache boxes just introduced. Transition: the research system becomes an Apache project and develops higher-level APIs.

Advance once to travel to the next milestone. The rail uses milestone spacing, not a proportional elapsed-time scale.

Sources (checked 2026-09-18):
https://research.google/pubs/the-google-file-system/
https://research.google/pubs/mapreduce-simplified-data-processing-on-large-clusters/
